{
  "target": "src.api.report_view:ReportView",
  "diagnosis": "The class exhibits low cohesion (LCOM4=2) because its methods operate on two distinct sets of attributes: `title` and `describe_customer` rely on `self._width`, while `order_count` and `mail_summary` rely on `self._repository` and `self._mailer`. Additionally, `describe_customer` demonstrates feature envy by accessing four attributes of the `Customer` object, suggesting that the formatting logic belongs closer to the data source or a dedicated formatter, rather than in the view which only holds a width constraint.",
  "suggestions": [
    {
      "title": "Extract customer formatting logic",
      "rationale_metric_link": [
        "LCOM4",
        "DCC"
      ],
      "expected_effect": [
        {
          "metric": "LCOM4",
          "direction": "down",
          "confidence": 0.9
        },
        {
          "metric": "DCC",
          "direction": "same",
          "confidence": 0.8
        }
      ],
      "sketch": "Move the logic in `describe_customer` to a method on the `Customer` class (e.g., `Customer.format_for_report(width)`) or a dedicated `CustomerFormatter` class. This removes the dependency of `ReportView` on the internal structure of `Customer` and isolates the `self._width` usage. If moved to `Customer`, `ReportView` simply calls `customer.format_for_report(self._width)`. This reduces the attribute coupling within `ReportView` by removing the need for `ReportView` to know how to assemble customer parts, potentially allowing `describe_customer` to be removed or simplified to a pure delegation, which helps consolidate the method-attribute graph."
    },
    {
      "title": "Separate reporting and notification responsibilities",
      "rationale_metric_link": [
        "LCOM4",
        "DCC"
      ],
      "expected_effect": [
        {
          "metric": "LCOM4",
          "direction": "down",
          "confidence": 0.7
        },
        {
          "metric": "DCC",
          "direction": "down",
          "confidence": 0.6
        }
      ],
      "sketch": "Split `ReportView` into two classes: `ReportFormatter` (handling `title`, `describe_customer`, and `self._width`) and `ReportNotifier` (handling `mail_summary` and `order_count`, depending on `repository` and `mailer`). This eliminates the LCOM4 warning by ensuring each new class has a single cohesive set of attributes. `ReportFormatter` would have DCC=0 (or 1 if it references Customer), and `ReportNotifier` would have DCC=2 (Repository, Mailer). This adheres to the Single Responsibility Principle."
    }
  ],
  "risk_notes": "Splitting the class increases the number of classes in the system and may require updating the composition root where `ReportView` is instantiated. Moving logic to `Customer` requires ensuring `Customer` is not a simple data transfer object (DTO) if the project architecture forbids behavior on DTOs. The `mail_summary` method currently returns a dict from the mailer; if this return value is used for UI feedback, separating it from the view might require a new interface or event mechanism."
